## 14. Linear Search vs Binary Search

| Feature | Linear Search | Binary Search |
|---|---|---|
| Data requirement | can be unsorted | must be sorted |
| Method | check one by one | check middle and halve range |
| Simple to write | yes | more complex |
| Good for small data | yes | also works, but sorting may not be worth it |
| Good for large sorted data | less efficient | more efficient |
| Works on linked list easily | yes | not usually ideal |
| Best case | first item found | middle item found |
| Worst case | checks all items | repeatedly halves until range empty |

### Mark Scheme Phrase

```text
Linear search is simpler and works on unsorted data, while binary search is faster for large sorted lists but requires sorted data.
```

